1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,930

2
00:00:05,930 --> 00:00:06,540
All right.

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:07,290
So hi, everyone.

4
00:00:07,290 --> 00:00:08,840
We're going to get started.

5
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:12,590
So for today's lecture
for CS25, very pleasure

6
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:14,480
to have Denny Zhao from
Google DeepMind here

7
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:17,520
to give a talk on large
language model reasoning.

8
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:19,880
And so Denny founded
the reasoning team

9
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:23,610
at Google Brain, which is
now part of Google DeepMind.

10
00:00:23,610 --> 00:00:26,600
His group is renowned for
pioneering chain-of-thought

11
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:29,120
prompting and
self-consistency, as well as

12
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:31,490
developing the
mathematical foundations

13
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:34,970
of in-context learning and
chain-of-thought reasoning.

14
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:37,130
His team also created
core capabilities

15
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:40,500
that power Gemini's
reasoning capabilities.

16
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:42,710
Further, Denny
co-founded the conference

17
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:46,880
on language modeling, or COMM,
and served as general chair

18
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:49,730
for the 2024 conference.

19
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:52,950
So yeah, I'll let Denny
take it from here.

20
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:56,300

21
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:58,010
[APPLAUSE]

22
00:00:58,010 --> 00:01:01,930
I'm glad to see many of
you guys have already

23
00:01:01,930 --> 00:01:04,480
believed LLMs can reason.

24
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:07,650
Actually, you may wonder what
my answer for this question.

25
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:11,590

26
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:12,230
Yeah.

27
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:16,270
To me, actually, I don't know.

28
00:01:16,270 --> 00:01:21,040
That really depends on the
definition of reasoning.

29
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:26,380
So for my talk today, I will
have a very specific definition

30
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:27,670
about reasoning.

31
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:31,400
So I know there's many debates
about if LLM's can reason.

32
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:35,080
I never join those
debates because

33
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:38,230
without a definition
on reasoning,

34
00:01:38,230 --> 00:01:40,690
I have no idea
about those things.

35
00:01:40,690 --> 00:01:47,860
But for LLM reasoning,
we particularly

36
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:54,790
mean that intermediate tokens
between input and output

37
00:01:54,790 --> 00:01:59,840
that are called reasoning
are our intermediate steps.

38
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:03,680
So this idea actually
is not very new.

39
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:09,080
Even in 2017, DeepMind
already published a paper,

40
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:14,210
How to use Intermediate
Tokens to Solve Math Problems.

41
00:02:14,210 --> 00:02:19,490
So at that time, I think the
community were quite hyped

42
00:02:19,490 --> 00:02:21,560
about AlphaGo, Alphazero.

43
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:25,320
But this paper is a really
groundbreaking paper.

44
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:27,360
If you haven't read
that paper before,

45
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:31,550
I strongly encourage you
to look at that paper.

46
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:38,090
They introduced natural
language to solve math problems.

47
00:02:38,090 --> 00:02:41,160
However, in the
literature at that time,

48
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:48,830
I think everyone else just used
the symbolic approach or search.

49
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:50,720
So actually, this
idea actually is also

50
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:55,660
very common for neural
symbolic literature.

51
00:02:55,660 --> 00:02:57,970
In neurosymbolic
literature, actually, it's

52
00:02:57,970 --> 00:03:03,370
very common to use intermediate
process to solve some reasoning

53
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:04,220
problems.

54
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:11,470
Here's an example about
how to use LLM reasoning.

55
00:03:11,470 --> 00:03:15,110
When I founded the original
team in Google Brain,

56
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:18,070
I created this task.

57
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:21,520
So it's called last
letter concatenation.

58
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,850
I use this task as a
motivating example.

59
00:03:24,850 --> 00:03:28,150
At that time, one could
use transformer models

60
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:30,070
to solve this task.

61
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:32,470
So what's the output
when concatenating

62
00:03:32,470 --> 00:03:37,000
the last letter of each word
of artificial intelligence?

63
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,590
So if there is no reasoning
process, you will say,

64
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:41,470
OK, the answer is LE.

65
00:03:41,470 --> 00:03:45,370
If there is a reasoning process,
the model would output, say--

66
00:03:45,370 --> 00:03:48,470
the last letter of
artificial is L.

67
00:03:48,470 --> 00:03:53,260
The last letter of intelligence
is E. Concatenating L and E

68
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:55,690
leads to LE, or
something like that.

69
00:03:55,690 --> 00:04:00,000
So the highlighted text
here is called reasoning.

70
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,440
That's what I particularly
mean here about reasoning.

71
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:08,490
So if you are familiar
with program synthesis

72
00:04:08,490 --> 00:04:11,100
or neural symbolic
reasoning, you

73
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:14,920
wouldn't be surprised
about this task design.

74
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:18,640
Of course, you can imagine
that I tried other options.

75
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:22,650
For example, I didn't
see the first letter.

76
00:04:22,650 --> 00:04:25,350
The reason is that I
tried first letter,

77
00:04:25,350 --> 00:04:27,720
and all language models
can solve that problem

78
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:31,900
quite well because there are
so many initiatives on the web,

79
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:33,660
and the model has
already learned how

80
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:35,940
to concatenate first letters.

81
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:40,070
Then I switched to last letters
and tthe old models failed.

82
00:04:40,070 --> 00:04:48,150

83
00:04:48,150 --> 00:04:51,570
I know many people say, oh
yeah, this is so natural, right?

84
00:04:51,570 --> 00:04:56,230
We need intermediate
steps, just like humans.

85
00:04:56,230 --> 00:04:58,270
I know in the
current days, you may

86
00:04:58,270 --> 00:05:01,510
see LLMs are very
similar to humans.

87
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:07,030
But for us as researchers, we
should always keep in mind,

88
00:05:07,030 --> 00:05:09,440
LLMs are just
probability models.

89
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:11,200
They are not humans.

90
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:13,880
And if you always
keep this in mind,

91
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:15,610
it will be better
for you to understand

92
00:05:15,610 --> 00:05:18,970
a lot of new techniques.

93
00:05:18,970 --> 00:05:23,540
So why intermediate tokens
or reasoning matters?

94
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:24,340
OK.

95
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:27,130
We have theoretical
work, actually

96
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:29,740
collaborated with
Professor Tuyima

97
00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:32,440
in Stanford and his students.

98
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:38,890
So for any problems solvable
by Boolean circuits of size t,

99
00:05:38,890 --> 00:05:42,190
constant-size
transformers can solve it

100
00:05:42,190 --> 00:05:45,880
by generating O(T)
intermediate tokens.

101
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:47,950
This is a very powerful result.

102
00:05:47,950 --> 00:05:54,120
So the size here means
the number of logic gates.

103
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:58,060
So for example, if you
use a GPU clusters,

104
00:05:58,060 --> 00:06:01,260
that would be tens of
millions case, right?

105
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:02,860
Even billions, trillions.

106
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:03,720
Yeah.

107
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:06,730
If we directly generate
the final answers,

108
00:06:06,730 --> 00:06:12,660
either we require a huge
depth or cannot solve at all.

109
00:06:12,660 --> 00:06:16,470
That's how we
understand reasoning

110
00:06:16,470 --> 00:06:20,580
from a theoretical perspective.

111
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:24,180
In the latter of this
lecture, I will come back

112
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:25,670
to this theoretical argument.

113
00:06:25,670 --> 00:06:30,120

114
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:34,200
There is a common belief
about LLM reasoning.

115
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:40,770
And pre-trained LLMs cannot
reason without further prompting

116
00:06:40,770 --> 00:06:44,100
engineering like code
prompting or fine tuning.

117
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:48,140
In the current days, everyone
talks about AI fine tuning,

118
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:50,300
right?

119
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:51,650
Is that true?

120
00:06:51,650 --> 00:06:53,120
Do everyone agree with that?

121
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:53,970
You agree with that?

122
00:06:53,970 --> 00:06:57,350

123
00:06:57,350 --> 00:06:58,880
OK.

124
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:01,320
So I believe it's wrong.

125
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:03,650
Yeah.

126
00:07:03,650 --> 00:07:04,470
It's very wrong.

127
00:07:04,470 --> 00:07:06,440
Yeah.

128
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:09,830
So pre-trained LLMs
are ready to reason,

129
00:07:09,830 --> 00:07:11,870
and all we need is a decoding.

130
00:07:11,870 --> 00:07:13,680
Just about the decoding process.

131
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:14,352
Yeah.

132
00:07:14,352 --> 00:07:17,240
No matter how fine
those techniques looks

133
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:18,660
like in [INAUDIBLE].

134
00:07:18,660 --> 00:07:22,520

135
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:24,600
So here is an example here.

136
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:28,820
If I have three apples, my dad
has two more apples than me,

137
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:31,435
how many apples do
we have in total?

138
00:07:31,435 --> 00:07:35,450

139
00:07:35,450 --> 00:07:41,310
If you have any pre-trained
models like Lama,

140
00:07:41,310 --> 00:07:45,140
DeepSeek or [INAUDIBLE]
or something--

141
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:46,440
I didn't try those models.

142
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:46,980
OK.

143
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:48,880
If you have any
pre-trained models,

144
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:51,630
you can type this question
in the pre-trained model

145
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:52,960
and see what happens.

146
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:56,320
Probably it's very likely you
see answer like five apples.

147
00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:57,880
Of course, the
answer is wrong here.

148
00:07:57,880 --> 00:08:00,300
This is called a
gradient decoding.

149
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:02,080
You will say, OK,
yeah, you're right.

150
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:06,450
For pre-trained models,
there is no reasoning, right?

151
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:10,140
The problem is about decoding
because the way you also decode

152
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:12,630
gradient decoding by default.

153
00:08:12,630 --> 00:08:16,350
If you look at the
second candidate--

154
00:08:16,350 --> 00:08:17,490
if you look at the--

155
00:08:17,490 --> 00:08:20,220
because you have big
vocabulary size, right?

156
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:22,570
And you can look at
the second candidate--

157
00:08:22,570 --> 00:08:24,940
a second candidate
for the first token.

158
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:28,990
And probably we start from
I and we see what happened.

159
00:08:28,990 --> 00:08:32,620
We just then continue
the decoding process.

160
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:36,299
We see, OK, I have three
apples and then my dad

161
00:08:36,299 --> 00:08:38,140
has two more apples than me.

162
00:08:38,140 --> 00:08:42,960
So he has five apples
and 3 plus 5 equal to 8.

163
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:44,610
It's perfect, right?

164
00:08:44,610 --> 00:08:46,950
We just need to look
for more candidates.

165
00:08:46,950 --> 00:08:49,460
That's amazing.

166
00:08:49,460 --> 00:08:52,410
And there's another choice.

167
00:08:52,410 --> 00:08:55,380
And the third candidate
for the first token is we.

168
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:57,170
We see what happens here.

169
00:08:57,170 --> 00:08:59,730
We have eight apples in total.

170
00:08:59,730 --> 00:09:00,230
Yes.

171
00:09:00,230 --> 00:09:00,660
Yeah.

172
00:09:00,660 --> 00:09:01,160
That's it.

173
00:09:01,160 --> 00:09:02,960
Somehow it's always cracked.

174
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:05,870
And [INAUDIBLE] probably
from the fourth candidate

175
00:09:05,870 --> 00:09:06,740
would be you.

176
00:09:06,740 --> 00:09:08,010
We will continue decoding.

177
00:09:08,010 --> 00:09:10,620
We'll see what
happened here again.

178
00:09:10,620 --> 00:09:11,690
Yeah.

179
00:09:11,690 --> 00:09:15,780
You can clearly see a chain
of thought in this response.

180
00:09:15,780 --> 00:09:17,590
And the final answer is correct.

181
00:09:17,590 --> 00:09:20,990

182
00:09:20,990 --> 00:09:25,940
And this is the fifth
candidate for the first token.

183
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:26,900
And the answer is 5.

184
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:27,450
It's wrong.

185
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:27,950
OK.

186
00:09:27,950 --> 00:09:28,910
Yeah.

187
00:09:28,910 --> 00:09:32,510
You can see that actually,
the reasoning part is already

188
00:09:32,510 --> 00:09:34,450
in the output space.

189
00:09:34,450 --> 00:09:37,550

190
00:09:37,550 --> 00:09:43,250
And in particular here,
for the second response

191
00:09:43,250 --> 00:09:45,850
and the fourth
first response, they

192
00:09:45,850 --> 00:09:50,840
are based on
chain-of-thought reasoning.

193
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:54,010

194
00:09:54,010 --> 00:09:57,940
The problem is how to select
the best response, right?

195
00:09:57,940 --> 00:10:00,110
If you just look at
the examples here,

196
00:10:00,110 --> 00:10:05,140
you may see, OK, we
can by output lengths.

197
00:10:05,140 --> 00:10:09,400
If the model has some
thinking, the output length

198
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:13,813
will be longer because it
contains reasoning tokens.

199
00:10:13,813 --> 00:10:16,930

200
00:10:16,930 --> 00:10:24,190
And actually we have a better
idea to select the response

201
00:10:24,190 --> 00:10:27,760
and by its answer confidence.

202
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:31,960
Confidence means because
the model is just

203
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:36,910
a probabilistic model, we
can look the probability

204
00:10:36,910 --> 00:10:40,840
of the token in prediction.

205
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:47,600
A very interesting thing is
that for the response with chain

206
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:53,386
of reasoning, the answer token
has way higher confidence.

207
00:10:53,386 --> 00:10:56,720

208
00:10:56,720 --> 00:11:01,070
For this example,
actually for the token 8,

209
00:11:01,070 --> 00:11:05,723
the model's confidence
is nearly 98%.

210
00:11:05,723 --> 00:11:08,360

211
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:12,060
That's huge because we
have huge vocabulary size.

212
00:11:12,060 --> 00:11:15,430
So usually for each token
the probability is nearly 0.

213
00:11:15,430 --> 00:11:19,640

214
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:25,370
So this process is called a
chain-of-thought decoding.

215
00:11:25,370 --> 00:11:29,610
So basically it
consists of two steps.

216
00:11:29,610 --> 00:11:33,590
Step 1, is we just go
beyond greedy decoding

217
00:11:33,590 --> 00:11:38,420
by checking more
generation candidates.

218
00:11:38,420 --> 00:11:41,980
And in the second
step, we choose

219
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:44,680
candidates which
have the highest

220
00:11:44,680 --> 00:11:46,605
confidence on the final answer.

221
00:11:46,605 --> 00:11:54,430

222
00:11:54,430 --> 00:11:58,803
And chain-of-thought decoding
is a very simple approach.

223
00:11:58,803 --> 00:12:01,930

224
00:12:01,930 --> 00:12:06,100
But still it needs
some programming work.

225
00:12:06,100 --> 00:12:09,040
And I heard in the countries
and the people just

226
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:10,510
want to use natural language.

227
00:12:10,510 --> 00:12:11,930
No one read the code.

228
00:12:11,930 --> 00:12:14,680
Of course, you guys
are exceptional.

229
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:18,430
And we have to say, OK, can
we reshape the model's output

230
00:12:18,430 --> 00:12:26,980
distribution so that thoughtful
responses naturally rank first?

231
00:12:26,980 --> 00:12:29,830
If the chain-of-thought
response is

232
00:12:29,830 --> 00:12:33,370
ranked first and then
the gradient decoding

233
00:12:33,370 --> 00:12:34,690
can naturally find it.

234
00:12:34,690 --> 00:12:40,590

235
00:12:40,590 --> 00:12:45,190
So now we can look at the
chain-of-thought prompting.

236
00:12:45,190 --> 00:12:47,500
If you know
chain-of-thought prompting,

237
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:51,990
now you can see why it works.

238
00:12:51,990 --> 00:12:54,720
Chain-of-thought prompting
is a very simple approach.

239
00:12:54,720 --> 00:12:58,830
So given this problem,
and you probably

240
00:12:58,830 --> 00:13:04,890
use another similar
problems as an example,

241
00:13:04,890 --> 00:13:09,450
and put that before
your question.

242
00:13:09,450 --> 00:13:12,300
And then the model
will magically

243
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:19,620
follow the reasoning
style and generate a step

244
00:13:19,620 --> 00:13:20,570
by step solution.

245
00:13:20,570 --> 00:13:23,220

246
00:13:23,220 --> 00:13:26,920
Yeah, now you can see that why
chain-of-thought prompting works

247
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:32,490
because it changes the
output distribution to push

248
00:13:32,490 --> 00:13:37,510
the original chain-of-thought
solutions in the output space

249
00:13:37,510 --> 00:13:40,570
to the top position.

250
00:13:40,570 --> 00:13:43,150
Even there is a simple
approach is called

251
00:13:43,150 --> 00:13:45,400
let's think step by step.

252
00:13:45,400 --> 00:13:51,070
There's another amazing
work in the reasoning.

253
00:13:51,070 --> 00:13:59,060
When that paper came out
I thought it was a joke.

254
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:00,500
How possible?

255
00:14:00,500 --> 00:14:02,650
Yeah.

256
00:14:02,650 --> 00:14:05,890
And at that time,
the Google Brain team

257
00:14:05,890 --> 00:14:08,650
built a model called PaLM.

258
00:14:08,650 --> 00:14:14,560
And I tried lessons that let's
step by step in our PaLM model

259
00:14:14,560 --> 00:14:19,990
because, of course, I
know how PaLM was built.

260
00:14:19,990 --> 00:14:23,330
It's definitely not related
to this magic trick.

261
00:14:23,330 --> 00:14:26,230
And then I found
it works on PaLM.

262
00:14:26,230 --> 00:14:28,480
I was so shocked.

263
00:14:28,480 --> 00:14:33,533
So this paper really inspired
me a lot on reasoning research.

264
